WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. This class turns the question,
what hardware do I

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
need, into a reproducible compatibility
and capacity decision. You will

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
bind the exact artifact to its runtime and
device stack,

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
estimate memory and storage without
mistaking estimates for measurements,

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
build a representative load profile, find
the first saturation point,

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
protect the host during overload, and
publish supported limits with

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
costs, exclusions, and re-benchmark
triggers.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.333
Begin with an exact-stack compatibility
matrix. Record model architecture,

9
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:45.667
artifact and tensor formats, precision or
quantization, tokenizer, template, maximum

10
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:51.000
tested context, runtime and version,
execution backend, operating system, CPU

11
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.333
architecture, accelerator model and count,
driver, compute library, container base,

12
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:01.667
and required extensions. Use current
runtime and hardware documentation to

13
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:07.000
identify a candidate combination, then
load the exact quarantined artifact

14
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.333
in the intended environment. Architecture
support does not prove support

15
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:17.667
for every conversion, quantization,
kernel, device, operating system, or

16
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:23.000
feature. Watch for automatic fallback. A
runtime may silently execute

17
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.333
one operation, or the entire model, on a
slower backend.

18
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:33.667
Make the selected backend visible in logs
and measurements. Accept

19
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:39.000
fallback only through an explicit quality,
latency, resource, and cost

20
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.333
decision. Label every matrix cell
documented, measured, inferred, or unknown

21
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:49.667
so a documentation claim is not presented
as a successful

22
00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:55.000
test.

23
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.154
Estimate before measuring so you can
reject impossible candidates safely.

24
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:07.308
Start with actual artifact sizes and a
rough weight-memory estimate

25
00:02:07.308 --> 00:02:13.462
from parameter count and bits per
parameter. Add quantization metadata,

26
00:02:13.462 --> 00:02:19.615
embeddings, runtime workspace, graph or
kernel caches, temporary buffers, loaded

27
00:02:19.615 --> 00:02:25.769
replicas, and safety headroom. Dynamic
cache depends on concurrent sequences,

28
00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:31.923
context and output lengths, cache
representation, batching, and runtime

29
00:02:31.923 --> 00:02:38.077
implementation. Add host memory for the
runtime, tokenizer, queues, telemetry,

30
00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:44.231
page cache, staging, and failure handling.
Add storage for immutable

31
00:02:44.231 --> 00:02:50.385
artifacts, container layers, caches, logs,
evaluation data, backups, and rollback

32
00:02:50.385 --> 00:02:56.538
bundles. Include download, verification,
load, compilation, and warm-up time. Every

33
00:02:56.538 --> 00:03:02.692
value is a hypothesis with an evidence
label and range.

34
00:03:02.692 --> 00:03:08.846
A model can fit at startup and still fail

35
00:03:08.846 --> 00:03:15.000
under long context, concurrency, batching,
restart, or recovery.

36
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.417
Use a deliberately rough synthetic
example. A candidate has eight

37
00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:25.833
billion parameters represented near four
bits per parameter. The simple

38
00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:31.250
weight estimate begins near four billion
bytes before metadata, scales,

39
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:36.667
padding, runtime copies, workspace, and
caches. Do not turn that

40
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:42.083
arithmetic into a device recommendation.
Add the measured artifact size,

41
00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:47.500
runtime overhead from the exact build,
context-cache estimates for short

42
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.917
and long requests, concurrent sequences,
one loaded replica, and a

43
00:03:52.917 --> 00:03:58.333
declared headroom target. The result is a
planning range. If

44
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:03.750
the high end exceeds usable device memory,
reject the scenario

45
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:09.167
or reduce model, context, concurrency, or
replicas before any load.

46
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:14.583
If it fits on paper, proceed to isolated
measurement. The

47
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:20.000
estimate filters candidates; it never
certifies capacity.

48
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.667
Version a representative load profile.
Include cold and warm starts,

49
00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:33.333
realistic input and output length
distributions, modalities, concurrency

50
00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:40.000
steps, bursts, cancellations, timeouts,
malformed requests, maximum allowed

51
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.667
context, pressure, restart, and recovery.
Measure time to ready, time

52
00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:53.333
to first output, inter-output latency when
relevant, end-to-end latency percentiles,

53
00:04:53.333 --> 00:05:00.000
throughput, queue time, error and
rejection rates, accelerator and host

54
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.667
memory, compute utilization, storage,
network, power where relevant, and restart

55
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:13.333
time. Keep artifact, runtime,
configuration, hardware, template, and

56
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:20.000
cases constant when comparing candidates.
Published benchmarks such as MLPerf

57
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.667
can inform methods and candidate
selection, but their workload, quality

58
00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:33.333
rules, software, and hardware describe
their own result. They do

59
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:40.000
not establish capacity for your request
distribution or service policy.

60
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Checkpoint. A published benchmark reports
high throughput on the same

61
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
accelerator family. Name three differences
that could make your endpoint's

62
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:01.000
capacity lower, and state the measurement
you still need.

63
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.714
A strong answer names the exact model and
precision, software

64
00:07:21.714 --> 00:07:27.429
stack, request-length distribution,
batching, concurrency, quality target,

65
00:07:27.429 --> 00:07:33.143
server limits, and full hardware
configuration. The benchmark remains

66
00:07:33.143 --> 00:07:38.857
useful evidence about its stated setup.
Your capacity requires the

67
00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:44.571
exact serving build under your versioned
representative profile. If your

68
00:07:44.571 --> 00:07:50.286
answer copied the throughput number
directly, replace it with a

69
00:07:50.286 --> 00:07:56.000
hypothesis and a measurement plan.

70
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.154
Increase concurrency and request size
gradually until latency, queueing, memory,

71
00:08:02.154 --> 00:08:08.308
errors, power, or quality crosses a
threshold declared in advance.

72
00:08:08.308 --> 00:08:14.462
Record the first limiting resource and
failure mode. Repeat after

73
00:08:14.462 --> 00:08:20.615
restart and with neighboring workloads
when resources are shared. Set

74
00:08:20.615 --> 00:08:26.769
container or scheduler requests and limits
from evidence while preserving

75
00:08:26.769 --> 00:08:32.923
host headroom for the operating system,
telemetry, recovery, and control

76
00:08:32.923 --> 00:08:39.077
plane. Docker notes that containers have
no resource constraints by

77
00:08:39.077 --> 00:08:45.231
default, and memory pressure can lead to
out-of-memory termination. Capacity

78
00:08:45.231 --> 00:08:51.385
therefore includes admission control,
bounded queues, context and output limits,

79
00:08:51.385 --> 00:08:57.538
rate or concurrency limits, deadlines,
cancellation, load shedding, stable

80
00:08:57.538 --> 00:09:03.692
overload responses, and bounded retry
guidance. An endpoint that accepts

81
00:09:03.692 --> 00:09:09.846
unlimited work until the host fails does
not have a

82
00:09:09.846 --> 00:09:16.000
capacity plan.

83
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:23.000
Publish a dated capacity decision. State
supported workload slices, tested

84
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:30.000
concurrency, latency and throughput
distributions, quality gates, resource

85
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:37.000
reservations and limits, minimum headroom,
overload behavior, replica assumptions,

86
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:44.000
startup and recovery time, storage, power
or cloud cost assumptions,

87
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:51.000
monitoring thresholds, and excluded cases.
Separate measurements from estimates and

88
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:58.000
extrapolation. Define re-benchmark
triggers: model revision, quantization,

89
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:05.000
runtime, driver, device, host, operating
system, container, context policy,

90
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:12.000
batching, template, feature flags, traffic
distribution, telemetry, or service

91
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:19.000
objective. Capacity evidence expires when
the production stack or workload

92
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:26.000
no longer matches what was measured.

93
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.667
Now build the measured-paper capacity
plan. Complete the exact-stack matrix,

94
00:10:32.667 --> 00:10:39.333
estimate three context and concurrency
scenarios, design cold, warm, steady,

95
00:10:39.333 --> 00:10:46.000
burst, boundary, overload, restart, and
recovery cases, declare thresholds before

96
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.667
viewing synthetic results, identify the
first saturated resource, and publish

97
00:10:52.667 --> 00:10:59.333
supported slices, headroom, costs,
overload behavior, exclusions, and

98
00:10:59.333 --> 00:11:06.000
re-benchmark triggers.

99
00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:11.000
Begin the check when you can identify the
full compatibility

100
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:16.000
stack, explain non-weight memory, choose a
representative load profile, design

101
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.000
overload behavior, bound external
benchmark claims, and recognize

102
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.000
re-benchmark triggers. The check starts
only when you choose Begin

103
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:31.000
knowledge check.

104
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.000
Remember: compatibility is exact, sizing
begins as a hypothesis, capacity

105
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
comes from representative measurement, and
every supported limit expires when

106
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:46.000
its model, stack, workload, or objective
changes.
